
The system now has a new version of CCLX (the command language interpreter),
which permits the .LDF command to do more than just load Focal - now you can
specify an arbitrary set of Focal commands following the .LDF command, which
will be executed as soon as LDF is loaded.  The simplest idea is just to run
the program you want to use:

				.LDF L G myprog

but you can also use LDF to perform monitor-level operations that are not as
easily done with other programs - for example:

				.LDF T $H$J";L Q

will clear the screen.  The 'L Q' (Lib Quit) command returns to the monitor,
so the whole episode of loading Focal and executing these two commands looks
like a single monitor operation.  Putting this command string (sans the '.')
in a file called 'CS.CM' allows the '@CS' command to clear the screen.

Almost any separator can be used between the .LDF command and the Focal com-
mands; a space was used above, but ',', '/', '-', or ';'  work just as well.
